Send the ClientHello of a current Firefox, the one REALITY servers now demand.

xtls/reality of 2026-09-08 -- the revision Xray 26.9.9 ships -- rejects every
ClientHello that carries no X25519MLKEM768 key share before the optional
X25519 one (tls.go):

    if peerPub2 == nil {
        break // reject outdated/strange Client Hello that doesn't have X25519MLKEM768 before optional X25519
    }

It rejects it the quiet way REALITY always does: the connection is handed to
the camouflage site, the client gets a genuine certificate for it and reports
"REALITY authentication failed" although its public key, short id and SNI are
all correct. So when an operator updates Xray, every REALITY node dies at
once, while the very same links keep working through the Xray we ship beside
this core.

mihomo resolves fp=firefox to HelloFirefox_Auto, which in metacubex/utls
v1.8.7 (still the latest release) is Firefox 120 -- a profile from before that
key existed. `support-x25519mlkem768: true` does not help: it only stops the
core from stripping the key out of the hello, and there is nothing to strip.
Of the profiles in this fork only the chrome ones carry it, and chrome is the
fingerprint the censor filters where this client is used: 0 connections out of
30, against 30 out of 30 for firefox. Changing browsers is not a way out.

The spec is therefore brought in from refraction-networking/utls -- the fork
Xray uses, where HelloFirefox_Auto is already Firefox 148, which is why the
same server accepts Xray and refuses mihomo. One adjustment for this fork:
ReuseHybridAndClassicalKeyShares does not exist here, so the hybrid share and
the standalone X25519 share carry independent keys instead of the same one.
Nothing in the handshake depends on that repeat: the server reads the
standalone share, and that is exactly the key GetRealityConn derives auth_key
from.

Checked against a local REALITY server built on xtls/reality@2026-09-08: with
this patch fp=firefox completes the handshake, the firefox120 profile is
handed to the camouflage site and fails certificate verification there, and
chrome completes it as before.

Drop this patch once metacubex/utls ships a current firefox profile. Until
then keep the version in step with the uTLS profile of the bundled Xray: two
cores that answer to the same fp must look the same on the wire.

